--TEST--
Core\Jwt::sign writes `exp` and `iat` itself and refuses either one in $claims, and Core\Jwt::verify answers every claim the token carries — `rule:security/protocol-roster` and `rule:security/algorithm-comes-from-the-key`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Invariance over a sweep. `rule:security/algorithm-comes-from-the-key`'s second bullet says expiry is
// mandatory with "no flag to disable the check", and the way this class holds
// it is that `exp` is not the caller's to write at all: the lifetime is a
// positional `Duration` and the registered claims are the member's. So the
// property to assert is not that one round trip works — it is that *every*
// claim written comes back, and that the two the member adds are there on top,
// counted rather than read off a line.
secret bytes $key = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
array<string> $claims = ["sub" => "ada", "role" => "admin", "tenant" => "acme"];
string $token = Core\Jwt::sign($claims, 15m, $key);
array<tainted string> $back = Core\Jwt::verify($token, $key);

// Three claims in, five out: the caller's three unchanged, plus `iat` and
// `exp`. A member that dropped one, or that wrote the registered pair into a
// nested object, fails on the count while every individual line below still
// reads correctly.
echo Core\Arr::count($claims), " in, ", Core\Arr::count($back), " out", "\n";

int $survived = 0;
foreach ($claims as string $name => string $value) {
    if ($back[$name] == $value) {
        $survived = $survived + 1;
    }
}
echo $survived, " of ", Core\Arr::count($claims), " claims survive the round trip", "\n";

// The registered pair is present and ordered: a token is issued before it
// expires, and by exactly the lifetime that was asked for. Compared as text
// because that is what a claim is here — `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/jwt.rs`'s
// module doc owns why `rule:security/verification-does-not-launder`'s qualifier is what fixes the element
// type.
if (($back["exp"] as int) - ($back["iat"] as int) == 900) {
    echo "exp is iat plus the 15m that was asked for", "\n";
} else {
    echo "the lifetime did not reach the token", "\n";
}

// And neither registered name may be supplied. This is the whole difference
// between "the member checks for an expiry" and "the member owns the expiry":
// a caller who could write `exp` could write one in the past, or in the year
// 3000, and the check § 4 asks for would still pass.
try {
    string $own = Core\Jwt::sign(["sub" => "ada", "exp" => "253402300799"], 15m, $key);
    echo "a caller-supplied exp was accepted", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $setExpiry) {
    echo $setExpiry->message, "\n";
}
try {
    string $backdated = Core\Jwt::sign(["sub" => "ada", "iat" => "0"], 15m, $key);
    echo "a caller-supplied iat was accepted", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $setIssued) {
    echo $setIssued->message, "\n";
}

// A claim needs a name, so a list is refused rather than signed under the
// keys `0` and `1` — which is what an array whose keys are positional would
// otherwise become on the way through JSON.
try {
    string $positional = Core\Jwt::sign(["ada", "admin"], 15m, $key);
    echo "a positional claim was accepted", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $unnamed) {
    echo $unnamed->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
3 in, 5 out
3 of 3 claims survive the round trip
exp is iat plus the 15m that was asked for
Core\Jwt::sign(): $claims names `exp`, which this member writes from $lifetime — expiry is not the caller's to set, so that a token this member produces always carries one it will accept.
Core\Jwt::sign(): $claims names `iat`, which this member writes from $lifetime — expiry is not the caller's to set, so that a token this member produces always carries one it will accept.
Core\Jwt::sign(): $claims[0] has a positional key, and a claim has a name — write `["sub" => "ada"]` rather than a list.
